{
  "title": "The Death Years (Mahreen Sohail )",
  "date": "07-11",
  "text": "During the year that she was pregnant with me, my mother lost one of her uncles and her brother-in-law. They died in front of her; she saw their dead bodies. She was twenty-seven at the time. And then, after I was born as one of a set of twins, she lost my brother just a few hours after his birth. I think about the way death shadows my writing; and I wonder if some of the losses she faced while I was growing inside her were inherited by me. At times, I think about how much of my history my children will worry over. Will they sift through my life the way I want to sift through my mother’s past? The deaths that chased her the year she was pregnant eventually gave way to a period of happiness that lasted thirty-eight years, until my father’s death. This past year was the first year she forgot their wedding anniversary, seven years after he died.",
